Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing Markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex re(R"(?:(<p>.*?)</p>)");
    std::smatch match;
    while (std::regex_search(markdown, match, re)) {
        html = "<p>" + match[1] + "</p>";
    }
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a new paragraph.\n\nThis is a second paragraph.\n\nThis is a third paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to match Markdown paragraphs and replace them with HTML paragraphs. The regular expression `(?:(<p>.*?)</p>)` matches the text between `<p>` and `</p>` tags, which are the Markdown paragraphs. The `std::regex_search` function is used to find all matches and replace them with the corresponding HTML paragraphs.

